  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "First-Derivative Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the one-sided Laplace first-derivative property to replace $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}$ with $sX(s)-x(0^-)$ in Laplace-domain signal and system equations.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "First-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to First-Derivative Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided Laplace viewpoint first. We work on causal signals over $[0^-,\\infty)$, so the key transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To transform $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}$, define $u=e^{-st}$ and $dv=x'(t)dt$ and apply one-step integration by parts. Then treat the base causal case from this topic: if $x(t)=t$ for $t\\ge 0$, then $x(0^-)=0$ and the boundary correction does not appear as an extra nonzero constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st} \\, dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=\\left.x(t)e^{-st}\\right|_{0^-}^{\\infty}+s\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt=sX(s)-x(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the derivative in time maps to multiplication by $s$ in the $s$-domain, then you subtract the pre-initial value. In this one-sided form, the result is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read this as a template: whenever you see $x'(t)$ under a one-sided Laplace, write the $sX(s)$ term first, then always attach the correction $-x(0^-)$. The symbol $x(0^-)$ is the left-limit value at the causal boundary, not a post-origin value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "First-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Single First Derivative",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the Laplace transform of the derivative when $x(t)=t^2u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue from the prior section: when you see a first derivative, apply the one-sided identity to convert it into an algebraic $s$-domain form. Write it first as $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the boundary term for this signal. Since $x(t)=t^2u(t)$ is causal with value $0$ at the left of the origin, $x(0^-)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2u(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s^3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the transform pair into the rule and keep the correction term in the template form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)=s\\left(\\frac{2}{s^3}\\right)-0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for this step, the result is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s^2}$, and the only new wrinkle from the tutorial is that you now use a concrete $X(s)$ value before simplifying.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "First-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Scaled First-Derivative Term",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\{5x'(t)\\}$ when $x(t)=t^2u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this step is the scalar multiple: when the derivative is scaled by a constant, first transform the inner derivative and apply linearity to the whole result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $x(t)=t^2u(t)$, compute the one-sided boundary term first: $x(0^-)=0$. From the previous step, we already have $X(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2u(t)\\}=2/s^3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{5x'(t)\\}=5\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the one-sided derivative rule for $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}$ and preserve the initial-condition structure.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{5x'(t)\\}=5\\left(sX(s)-x(0^- )\\right)=5\\left(s\\cdot\\frac{2}{s^3}-0\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{5x'(t)\\}=\\frac{10}{s^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is $\\mathcal{L}\\{5x'(t)\\}=10/s^2$. This is exactly the same rule as before, with one extra wrinkle: a constant multiplier must scale the full derivative transform, including the subtraction term form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "First-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Replacing First Derivatives in a Laplace-Domain Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Transform $y'(t)+3y(t)=\\cos t\\,u(t)$ with $y(0^-)=2$ into an $s$-domain equation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this step is a differential equation that contains one derivative term and linear time-domain terms. Treat each term separately in the Laplace domain, then combine them into one algebraic equation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{y'(t)+3y(t)\\} = \\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos t\\,u(t)\\},",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the derivative, use the same cue-activated rule as before and keep the pre-initial term in place: $\\mathcal{L}\\{y'(t)\\}=sY(s)-y(0^-)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{y'(t)\\}=sY(s)-y(0^-)=sY(s)-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Transform the remaining left-hand and right-hand terms with linearity and the provided table identities.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{3y(t)\\}=3Y(s), \\quad \\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos t\\,u(t)\\}=\\frac{s}{s^2+1}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute these pieces into the transformed equation and collect unknown terms on the left.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(sY(s)-2)+3Y(s)=\\frac{s}{s^2+1}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s+3)Y(s)-2=\\frac{s}{s^2+1}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: the $s$-domain equation is $(s+3)Y(s)-2=s/(s^2+1)$. If a solved form is needed next, isolate $Y(s)$ as a single fraction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
